% dossier-function: original 12-bar ensemble theme and arranging study for Grade 8: three short scenes that put monophony, homophony and polyphony, and minor and major, in an order that tells a small story, compared with the sectional build of a film song
% dossier-elements: 12 bars in 4/4 in three four-bar scenes; Theme written range E4-E5, second line C4-C5; pitch set E F# G A B C D with D# and G# in bars 11-12; chords (from bar 5) G | Am | C | D | Em | Am-C | Am-B7 | E; rhythm built from one motif (quarter, dotted quarter, eighth, quarter) plus eighth runs, quarters, halves and a whole note; scene 1 (bars 1-4) the theme alone: E4 up to B4, an answering fall to F#4, then the motif reaching E4 to C5 and closing on B4 (open); scene 2 (bars 5-8) the motif in G major from G4, the second line a third or sixth below in the same rhythm, closing on D (V of G, still open); scene 3 (bars 9-12) the theme's motif from B4 to E5 over the second line's half notes E-G-A-G-E-F#, a running eighth bar (10), the leading tone D#5 over B7, and E5 over G#4 on E major; largest leaps the minor sixth E4-C5 (bar 3) and major sixth G4-E5 (bar 7), the theme's deliberate reach
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 100 is the target; start at 80. E minor is the rehearsal key (one sharp; the page's Transpose can move it to D minor for concert band). The Theme's written range E4-E5 sits a tone above the young-unison C4-D5 at its top only (E5 in bars 7, 9, 10 and 12), and the second line's C4-C5 sits inside it. Teach in steps: everyone learns the theme alone (scene 1), then half the class learns the second line for scene 2 by singing a third below, then scene 3's second line in half notes against the theme; finally the chord players add the bed and the class chooses the ending. Mute or hold back the Band in bars 1-4 so the opening stays one line
% dossier-accompaniment: The chord symbols begin at bar 5 so scene 1 stays monophonic; the page's ballad style (piano, soft kit, seventh chords) is a generic cinematic stand-in and not an Indian film-song arrangement. Chord players may take cue A in scene 2. No percussion pattern, string line, synth sound or vocal arrangement from "Jai Ho" is reproduced
% dossier-lyrics: Instrumental: recorders, bells, keyboards, ukuleles, strings or voices on "ah" for both lines. No words are supplied, and no words from Gulzar's "Jai Ho" lyric are used
% dossier-honesty: Original AI-assisted course composition generated for Grand Central Stationery on 11 November 2026 under human-directed range, texture and teaching constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community, not modelled on the reference's melody and not an imitation of Hindi film music; the listening reference is a bridge only
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the theme, second line, chords, improvisation cues and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. "Jai Ho", the film Slumdog Millionaire and their recordings remain their owners' copyright and are not included
% dossier-respect: Name the film (Slumdog Millionaire, directed by Danny Boyle, 2008, a British production set in Mumbai), A. R. Rahman as composer, Gulzar as lyricist and the singers, and the languages heard in the excerpt chosen. Explain that Rahman works across Tamil, Hindi and international cinema; do not use "Bollywood" as a catch-all for Indian music or present this classroom theme as Indian film music
