N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit of the Theme: bar 1 E4 quarter, B4 dotted quarter, A4 eighth, G4 quarter (the motif); bar 2 A-G-F#-E eighths, F#4 half; bar 3 the motif reaching E4 to C5, then B4, A4; bar 4 A-G-F#-A eighths, B4 half; bar 5 (G) the motif from G4: G4, D5, C5, B4; bar 6 (Am) C5-B-A-G eighths, A4 half; bar 7 (C) G4, E5, D5, C5; bar 8 (D) D5-C5-B4-C5 eighths, D5 half; bar 9 (Em) B4, E5, D5, B4; bar 10 (Am then C) running eighths C5-B-C-E5-D5-C-B-A; bar 11 (Am then B7) A-C-B-A eighths, B4 and D#5 quarters; bar 12 (E) E5 whole note. Second line: bars 1-4 rests; bars 5-8 D4-B4-A4-G4, A-G-F#-E-C4, E4-C5-B4-G4, F#-A-G-A-F#4, in the Theme's rhythm a third, fourth or sixth below; bars 9-12 half notes E4-G4, A4-G4, E4-F#4, then G#4 whole. Every bar of both parts totals eight eighths. Theme range E4-E5; second line C4-C5. The batch draft was eight single-line bars that claimed homophony, polyphony and dramatic form without a second part, a chord plan for scene 1 or any change of texture. Originality check: the motif is not the "Jai Ho" hook or chant, nor the opening of other Rahman themes checked ("Chaiyya Chaiyya", "Dil Se Re", "Kun Faya Kun"), and a rising-fifth opening with a stepwise fall is common property. The analyzer reads Grade 7 (triads beyond I, IV and V); the Grade 8 concept here, the three textures, lives in the part-writing, which the analyzer does not read.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether students hear three scenes in 12 bars at quarter note = 100, whether the minor sixth and major sixth reaches are comfortable for the class's instruments, and whether the band can be held back for scene 1 on the page have not been tested. An Indian film-music educator must review the framing and the excerpt chosen; a classroom trial, a second score reader and a student listening panel remain required before publication.
